
  [1mShort design: [38;5;153mtruncate[39m option for [38;5;153mformat()[0m

  [1mRecommendation: truncate at the last word boundary, falling back to a hard cut.[0m Your framing was "simpler vs better
  UX," but the gap in simplicity is smaller than it looks — the word-boundary version is the hard cut plus a
  [38;5;153mlastIndexOf(' ')[39m and one fallback branch, about four lines instead of one. Meanwhile mid-word cuts ([38;5;153m"Hello wor..."[39m)
  read as broken in exactly the display contexts truncation exists for. The cost is worth four lines.

  [1mOption shape:[0m [38;5;153mformat(str, { truncate: 20 })[39m — the number [3mis[0m the max length, matching the flat one-key-per-option
  pattern already in the file. Guarded with [38;5;153mtypeof options.truncate === 'number'[39m rather than the usual [38;5;153mif (options.X)[39m,
  so [38;5;153mtruncate: 0[39m means "empty" instead of silently doing nothing.

  [1mAlgorithm[0m, applied last, after [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m, per your call:

  1. String already within [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m → returned untouched, no ellipsis.
  2. [38;5;153mmaxLength <= 3[39m → return [38;5;153m'...'.slice(0, maxLength)[39m, so the cap holds even when there's no room for the ellipsis
     itself.
  3. Otherwise take the first [38;5;153mmaxLength - 3[39m characters, back off to the last space in that window, trim trailing
     whitespace, append [38;5;153m'...'[39m.
  4. No space in the window, or a space at index 0 (a long URL, one long word) → hard cut at [38;5;153mmaxLength - 3[39m plus [38;5;153m'...'[39m.

  [1mKnown tradeoff I'm choosing to accept:[0m word-boundary backoff can be aggressive. [38;5;153mformat("a verylongwordhere", { [39m
  [38;5;153mtruncate: 12 })[39m backs off to the space at index 1 and yields [38;5;153m"a..."[39m. The fix is a floor ("don't back off past half the
  window, hard-cut instead"), which I'm leaving out as YAGNI — say so and I'll add it.

